 "batch_notes": "(1) bitvec's 'Vec<bool> oracle' straddles external-oracle and model-oracle: I used external-oracle where the expected value is literally the same-named std slice method (reverse/rotate/chunks/windows/iteration, Ord on Vec<bool>) and model-oracle where the test computes the expectation itself (counting/searching, shifts, bit-op closures); a reviewer may prefer one bucket for all. (2) Recurring shape the rubric lacks: 'a documented resource limit is a tight bound' (boa loop/recursion limits, capnp nesting limit, capnp segment-table limits) - both accept-below and reject-above are asserted; classified spec-postcondition (or rejection when the reject side dominates). (3) Several categories depend on cargo features: borsh *_order_enforcement only rejects under de_strict_order (otherwise a by-construction decode check); bson hegel_single_byte_mutation_never_panics is only internal-consistency with the serde feature. (4) Data quality: many README lines say '(no doc comment)' although the property has a substantive comment (boa, brotli, bs58, capnp, bstr) - probably '//' rather than '///' comments; boa's loop_iteration_limit_is_a_tight_bound lists boa/1 twice in bugs_direct while the bug record's property is null; bson/1 has an empty test field and no pin in the batch. (5) calamine: get_row_column_never_panics is the second site of calamine/2 (title says two sites) but has no bugs_direct link; push_column_matches_column_number_to_name is documented as KNOWN FAILURE but its generator is not narrowed, so the pin tag there rests on the doc wording only. (6) The batch is heavy on roundtrip and robustness (calamine is almost entirely never-panics properties); no concurrency properties and nothing needed 'other'."
